\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Gradings, super-dimensions and indices}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{Let #{k} be a field with #{2\ne0}, and let #{V} be a
finite-dimensional #{k}-vector space. A \newvocab{grading} of #{V}
is an endomorphism #{\Gamma} with #{\Gamma^2=\bbI}, as in the
[massive graded Wilson data](fdip-00IN). Its eigenspaces
#{V_\pm=\ker(\Gamma\mp\bbI)} give #{V=V_+\oplus V_-}
by \ref{fdip-003O}. An endomorphism #{A} is
\newvocab{odd for a grading} when #{\Gamma A=-A\Gamma}.}

\p{For a subspace #{W} with #{\Gamma W\subseteq W}, the
projectors #{(\bbI\pm\Gamma)/2} restrict to #{W}. Its two
eigenspaces are #{W\cap V_+} and #{W\cap V_-}. Its
\newvocab{super-dimension} is the integer
##{\sdim W=\dim(W\cap V_+)-\dim(W\cap V_-).}
Its image in #{k} is #{\trace(\Gamma|_W)}, the supertrace of the
identity on #{W}, by the diagonal trace formula of \ref{fdip-00KG}
in bases of the two eigenspaces.}

\p{The \newvocab{generalized kernel} of #{A} is
#{\ker A^{\dim V}}. For a grading-stable kernel or generalized
kernel its \newvocab{index} is its super-dimension.}
